namespace Jazz2 {
//###==##====#=====--==~--~=~- --- -- -  -  -   -

/** @page editor Level editor
@brief The level editor built into the game, and the formats it reads and writes

@tableofcontents

@section editor-overview Overview

The game has a built-in level editor, compiled in with the `WITH_EDITOR` option @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see
@ref building-config-params-game) @m_endspan. It's on by default on every platform that can save files --- all except
the <b>Nintendo 64</b>, <b>Dreamcast</b>, <b>GameCube</b> and <b>PlayStation 2</b> --- but never in a dedicated server
or the Shareware Demo.

The editor works only with the game's own level format, not with original @cpp ".j2l" @ce files. Original levels are
converted on the first start @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref paths) @m_endspan and can be opened from @cpp "Cache/" @ce.

@section editor-opening Opening the editor

-   <b>Level Editor</b> in the main menu --- starts with an empty level and the <b>Open</b> dialog.
-   <b>Edit This Level</b> in the pause menu of a single-player game --- opens the current level.
-   The `/edit` command-line switch, optionally followed by a level (`episode/level`) or a file path:

@code{.sh}
Jazz2 /edit prince/01_castle1
Jazz2 /edit "C:\Levels\MyLevel.j2l"
@endcode

@section editor-interface Interface

-   <b>Menu bar</b> and <b>toolbar</b> at the top.
-   <b>Left panel</b> --- the tools, the current stamp, and the <b>Tiles</b> and <b>Events</b> pickers.
-   <b>Right panel</b> --- the layer list, and the properties of the selected layer or event below it.
-   <b>Status bar</b> --- how the current tool works (or what the gamepad buttons do), the current layer, the tile and
    event under the pointer, and the zoom.

The level fills the window behind the panels, and its music plays muffled, as under the pause menu.

Panels are resized by dragging their inner edges. Either panel can also float over the level
(@ref Editor::UiDockFrame) --- use the button in its header or drag the header by its grip. To dock it again, drag it
back to its edge, or double-click the header.

The layout follows the window size, or can be fixed to desktop or compact in the <b>View</b> menu:

-   narrower than 600 units, the panels become drawers opened by the buttons at the start of the toolbar,
-   narrower than 420 units, the tools move to a row at the bottom and the toolbar is trimmed.

<b>Expert Mode</b> in the <b>View</b> menu (on by default) shows all tools and commands; turned off, only the basic
ones. The interface scales with the window and can be made larger or smaller with
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>+</kbd> / <kbd>-</kbd>. Its text is drawn at @ref Editor::UiTheme::TextScale of
that scale.

In dialogs, <kbd>Enter</kbd> chooses the accented button, <kbd>Esc</kbd> or the cross cancels. Every RGB color has a
swatch that opens a <b>color picker</b> (@ref Editor::UiColorPicker) --- a saturation/value square, a hue bar, an alpha
bar where the color has one, and a `#RRGGBB` field. The swatch of the previous color brings it back.

@section editor-controls Controls

@subsection editor-controls-mouse Mouse

-   <b>Left button</b> --- use the tool. <b>Right button</b> --- pick the tile under the pointer (brush), or erase
    (rectangle, fill, events).
-   <b>Wheel</b> --- zoom at the pointer, in steps where every pixel of the level is the same size. With
    <kbd>Shift</kbd> it scrolls sideways, with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> up and down, with <kbd>Alt</kbd> it changes the layer
    and with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd> the interface scale.
-   <b>Middle button</b>, or the left one with <kbd>Space</kbd> held --- pan the view, with inertia.
-   <b>Number fields</b> --- drag, click to type, or use the arrows at their end (click or wheel, <kbd>Shift</kbd> for
    steps of ten).

Zoomed out until a tile is smaller than about 6 screen pixels, layers are drawn from cached images at 4 pixels per tile
(@ref Editor::TileLod), so even a large level is drawn quickly as a whole.

@subsection editor-controls-keyboard Keyboard

Keys | Action
---- | ------
<kbd>B</kbd>, <kbd>E</kbd>, <kbd>R</kbd>, <kbd>G</kbd>, <kbd>I</kbd> | Brush, eraser, rectangle, fill, eyedropper
<kbd>M</kbd>, <kbd>W</kbd>, <kbd>Q</kbd>, <kbd>H</kbd> | Select, magic wand, events, hand
<kbd>T</kbd>, <kbd>P</kbd>, <kbd>U</kbd>, <kbd>K</kbd> | Smart brush, ground line, measure, events along path
<kbd>Space</kbd> / <kbd>Alt</kbd> (held) | Temporary hand / eyedropper
<kbd>X</kbd> / <kbd>Y</kbd> | Flip the stamp horizontally / vertically
<kbd>Z</kbd> / <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> | Paint random tiles of the stamp / reroll them
<kbd>1</kbd> -- <kbd>9</kbd> | Edit a layer, with <kbd>Shift</kbd> hide or show it
<kbd>Page Up</kbd> / <kbd>Page Down</kbd> | Previous / next layer
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Y</kbd> | Undo / redo
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>X</kbd>, <kbd>C</kbd>, <kbd>V</kbd> | Cut, copy, paste --- pasted tiles become the brush stamp
<kbd>Del</kbd> | Delete the selection
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>A</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>D</kbd> | Select all / deselect
<kbd>Esc</kbd> | Cancel the current tool action, then deselect
<kbd>+</kbd>, <kbd>-</kbd> | Zoom in, out
<kbd>0</kbd> / <kbd>Home</kbd> | Zoom as in the game / fit the whole level
Arrows | Move the view
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>G</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd> | Toggle grid / flat layers
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd> | Pause layers that move by themselves
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>E</kbd> | Show events while using other tools
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>M</kbd> | Cycle collision masks: over the tiles, instead of them, off (see @ref editor-layers)
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd> | Show where the player can get
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>T</kbd> | Edit level texts
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd> | Find and replace tiles or events
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Right</kbd> | Continue the row right of the pointer with suggested tiles
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>N</kbd>, <kbd>O</kbd>, <kbd>S</kbd> | New, open, save
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>E</kbd> | Save as / export
<kbd>F5</kbd>, <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F5</kbd>, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F5</kbd> | Play, play here, play from start --- <kbd>F5</kbd> again returns
<kbd>F6</kbd> / <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>F5</kbd> | Set the play-start marker at the pointer / play from it
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>L</kbd> | Toggle the path of the last play-test
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>1</kbd> -- <kbd>9</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>1</kbd> -- <kbd>9</kbd> | Set a bookmark at the view center / go to it
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>N</kbd> | Add a note at the pointer
<kbd>F2</kbd> | Navigator --- the whole level in small, with bookmarks and notes; click or drag to move there
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>H</kbd> | History --- click a change to go back or forward to it
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> | Command palette --- search all commands by name, <kbd>Enter</kbd> runs the first match
<kbd>F1</kbd> | Keyboard shortcuts

To change a shortcut, open <b>Keyboard Shortcuts</b> (<kbd>F1</kbd>), click it and press the new keys ---
<kbd>Backspace</kbd> removes it, <kbd>Esc</kbd> keeps it. An action that had the same shortcut loses it. Changes are
saved in @cpp "Jazz2.EditorHotkeys.json" @ce in the configuration directory as an object of action identifiers, each
@cpp [key, modifiers] @ce or @cpp null @ce (@ref Editor::ActionRegistry::LoadOverrides()). <b>Reset All</b> restores the
defaults.

Copied tiles and events also go to the system clipboard as text starting with @cpp {"J2R": @ce, so they can be pasted
into another level or another running instance of the game.

Bookmarks and notes are editor-only: they're stored in the @cpp "anno" @ce chunk (see @ref editor-formats-chunks),
dropped on export, and not undoable, though changing them marks the level unsaved (@ref Editor::Annotation).

@subsection editor-controls-gamepad Gamepad

Pressing any gamepad button shows a cursor and lists the buttons in the status bar. Using the mouse or keyboard switches
back.

Gamepad | Action
------- | ------
Left stick | Move the cursor --- faster the further and longer it's pushed
Directional pad | Move the cursor by one tile; over a number, change it
Right stick | Move the view; over a list, scroll it
Left / right trigger | Zoom out / in at the cursor
A | Use the tool, or press what the cursor is over
B | Erase, or close what's open over the interface
X | Pick the tile or event under the cursor
Y | Next tool; hold for a tool ring (aim with the left stick, release to pick); over a text field, open a keyboard
Left / right bumper | Previous / next layer
Back / left stick press | Undo / redo
Start | Play

Text is typed with the system's on-screen keyboard where there is one, otherwise with the editor's own
(@ref Editor::UiOnScreenKeyboard).

@subsection editor-controls-touch Touch screen

The status bar lists the gestures (@ref Editor::TouchGestures).

Fingers | Action
------- | ------
One | Use the tool once the finger moves a little (so a second finger can still turn it into a gesture); a quick tap is a click. Over the interface, it acts as a pointer
Two | Pan and pinch to zoom
Tap with two / three | Undo / redo
Hold one still | Tool ring, tap to pick
Tap a text field | System keyboard, or the editor's own

@section editor-layers Layers

The layer list is in the right panel:

-   The <b>eye</b> hides a layer and the <b>lock</b> protects it --- in the editor only, the level isn't changed.
-   Drag a layer to reorder it, double-click to rename it. An unnamed layer shows its kind, dimmed. Names are
    editor-only and dropped on export.

Layer properties, below the list:

-   <b>Speed</b> and <b>offset</b>, and a <b>model</b> per axis --- moving by its speed, fixed to the screen, fitted to
    the level, or moving by the view size.
-   <b>Movement</b> presets set speeds and models for common layer kinds at once (foreground, background depths,
    fixed, fitted), and show <em>Custom</em> otherwise.
-   <b>Renderer</b> --- tiles, solid color, tinted, x-ray, blur behind, sky or circle.
-   <b>Color</b>, whose meaning depends on the renderer: a multiplier for tiles, a palette entry and strength for a
    tinted layer, or the horizon and stars for a sky or circle. A sky or circle is shown as plain tiles while edited.

<b>Resize Layer</b> in the <b>Layer</b> menu changes the size around an anchor point, and fills the new area with empty
tiles, the extended edge tiles, or the layer repeated. Events and the water level follow the sprite layer.

How layers are shown:

-   Layers scroll with parallax, placed as the game would place them for a player at the view center.
    <b>Flat Layers</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>) aligns them all to one grid, which helps when editing backgrounds.
-   A layer that moves by itself stays still while edited. <b>Pause Layer Movement</b>
    (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>) stops all of them.
-   Outside the edited layer is darkened where it doesn't repeat. A repeating layer shows a frame at its real extent and
    fainter lines where it repeats.
-   <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>M</kbd> shows the collision masks of the edited layer, darkened and outlined. On the sprite
    layer, a symbol shows what an event makes the tile do --- one-way, vine, hook, pole, hurting, instant death,
    destructible, collapsing, ricochet, slide, no climbing, tube or trigger.

@section editor-level Level properties

In the <b>Level</b> menu:

-   <b>Properties</b> --- name, author, next, secret and bonus levels, music, starting light, weather, water level, the
    caption tile, and the pit and multiplayer flags.
    -   The <b>author</b> defaults to the player name from the options (<b>Me</b> puts it back). The custom level list
        shows it after the level name.
    -   Level links pick a file; a level of the same episode is stored without the episode name, as in original levels.
    -   <b>Music</b> is picked from the `Music` directories of the user content, `Content` or the original game, and
        starts playing right away.
    -   The <b>caption tile</b> is shown in the level list and lights up RGB keyboards during play. The brush button
        takes the brush tile, <b>Auto</b> picks a colorful tile the level uses a lot.
-   <b>Texts</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>T</kbd>) --- events refer to texts by number, so texts can only be added or
    removed at the end. <kbd>Enter</kbd> starts a new line, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> confirms. Formatting codes
    are shown as `\f`, a backslash as `\\`.

When the level is saved, a small preview of its start --- all layers with game parallax, a third of the size --- is
stored with it (the @cpp "PRVW" @ce chunk, see @ref Formats::LevelFile::PreviewChunk). The custom level list shows it
for the selected level.

@section editor-tiles Tiles

@subsection editor-tiles-picker Tile picker

-   Click picks a tile, drag picks a rectangle.
-   The arrows next to the name switch between tile sets and animated tiles.
-   <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+wheel or the buttons on the right change the tiles per row --- 10 by default, the width the original
    tile sets were drawn in.
-   With the brush, the <b>Next</b> row suggests tiles that other levels put next to the tiles around the pointer.
    <b>Continue Row by Suggestions</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Right</kbd>) fills the empty cells to the
    right of the pointer with the most common ones.
-   The magnifier button in the header shows tile usage: unused tiles are dark, used ones have a bar by how often.

@subsection editor-tiles-edit Editing a tile

Double-click a tile in the picker, or use <b>Edit Tile of the Brush</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu, to change its
<b>pixels</b> and collision <b>mask</b>. The change applies only to this level; the tile set file stays as it is.

-   Tools: pencil, line, rectangle (filled with <kbd>Shift</kbd>), fill, <b>Replace</b> (recolors every pixel of the
    clicked color), selection (drag to move, right button to clear) and eyedropper. Left paints, right erases.
-   Buttons below flip, rotate and shift the whole tile --- pixels and mask together, wrapping around.
-   The edges of neighboring tiles in the tile set are shown dimmed around it.
-   <b>Paint in Level</b> paints directly in the level: every place the edited layer shows the tile is outlined, and
    painting any of them changes all of them (@ref Editor::TilePixelTool).
-   Mask: <b>Mask from Pixels</b> (solid where opaque), <b>Fill Below Surface</b> (solid below a line drawn with the
    pencil), <b>Slope</b> (between heights at the left and right edge), <b>Mask of Brush Tile</b>, <b>Empty</b>,
    <b>Solid</b>.
-   <b>As in Tile Set</b> drops the changes.
-   <b>Export PNG</b> / <b>Import PNG</b> exchange the tile with another program. Only the top-left 32 × 32 pixels are
    read; images in the level palette keep their indices, other colors are mapped to the nearest entry.

The level stores palette indices, so a true color tile is reduced to the nearest palette colors --- unless it belongs to
the tiles of this level.

@subsection editor-tiles-level Tiles of this level

The <b>tiles of this level</b> are an extra tile set stored inside the level, not in any file
(@ref Formats::ExtraTileSetRecord::Embedded). It's created when tiles are first added, then listed with the other extra
tile sets.

-   Its tiles are edited in <b>true color</b> with transparency: the color picker has alpha, the palette offers its
    colors too, and imported images keep their colors.
-   <b>Copy into This Level</b> in the window of any other tile copies it there, and the level uses the copy wherever
    it used the tile --- one undo step. This allows true color changes to any tile, per level, which the original
    game's 8-bit overrides can't do.
-   Undo removes added tiles. Replacing this tile set with another drops its tiles; <b>Save Tile Changes into Tile
    Set</b> moves them into a file.

@subsection editor-tiles-animated Animated tiles

<b>Animated Tiles</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu lists them, with the selected one on the right.

-   A new animated tile is made of the brush tiles. <b>Add Brush</b> inserts them as frames after the selected frame;
    the window stays open while tiles are picked.
-   Frame time is in game frames (60 per second). The delay holds the last frame longer, and an animation can play
    forward and back.
-   A removed animated tile is replaced by its first frame everywhere. <b>Paint with It</b> makes it the brush.

@subsection editor-tiles-sets Tile sets

All in the <b>Level</b> menu. New or changed tile set files are saved into the user content as
`Tilesets/<name>.j2t`. A tile set that isn't there yet is copied there first, and the copy is used from then on.

-   <b>Tile Sets</b> --- the default tile set and the <b>extra tile sets</b>, whose tiles follow the default one's. An
    extra tile set can use a range of its file's tiles, and can have its colors matched to the level palette (otherwise
    its indices are used as they are). The level's tiles are remapped on every change, so they keep showing the same
    picture; tiles of a removed set, or outside a range, are cleared. All of it is one undo step. Tile IDs grow as
    needed, but scripts can't address more than 4096.
-   <b>New Tile Set</b> --- empty tiles in the level palette, added as an extra or the default tile set.
-   <b>New Tile Set from Image</b> --- the image is cut into 32 × 32 tiles row by row.
    -   Colors: keep the image's own palette (indexed images), keep true color, map to the level palette, or
        <b>generate a palette</b>. A generated palette keeps the entries sprites use, so they still look right, and
        fills the rest from the image (@ref Editor::BuildPalette() --- median cut refined by k-means).
    -   <b>Dither</b> mixes in-between colors with an ordered pattern that continues across tiles.
    -   <b>Skip tiles that are the same as another</b> drops duplicates, empty tiles included.
    -   An image `.mask.png` next to it gives the masks; otherwise tiles are solid where they aren't transparent.
    -   The first tile is always empty, trailing empty tiles are dropped. The name can't be one that already exists.
    -   An image from <b>Export Tile Set Image</b>, edited elsewhere, comes back unchanged this way.
-   <b>Add Tiles from Image</b> --- cuts an image the same way and appends it to a tile set of the level in its
    nearest colors, optionally skipping tiles it already has. Existing tiles keep their places. <b>Add to</b> can also
    put them into a <b>New tile set</b> or into the <b>Tiles of this level</b>.
-   <b>Save Tile Changes into Tile Set</b> (also <b>Save into Tile Set</b> in a tile's window) --- moves this level's
    tile changes into the tile set they came from, where every level using it sees them, or into a new tile set this
    level then uses. True color tiles stay true color; remapped extra tile sets get their own indices back. Undo puts
    the changes back into the level, but the saved file stays.
-   <b>Rearrange Tile Set</b> (@ref Editor::ShowTileSetLayoutDialog()) --- deletes and moves tiles of the default tile
    set and inserts empty ones. Levels refer to tiles by position, so either:
    -   save it as itself --- every writable level using it is updated, and levels in the game files are listed, since
        they'd show the wrong tiles; or
    -   save it as a new tile set used only by this level.

    Deleted tiles are cleared from the level. Changes to other files can't be undone.
-   <b>Export Tile Set Image</b> --- saves the default tile set as a PNG in rows of 10, plus `.mask.png` with the
    masks. 8-bit tile sets are saved indexed in their own palette with entry 0 transparent, losslessly. The editor has
    its own PNG codec (@ref Editor::PngCodec) and reads every kind of PNG.
-   <b>Generate Effect Tiles</b> --- tiles for a Blur Behind layer: clear glass, glass blocks, frosted glass, water,
    heat haze, a magnifier or a puddle, of a given size and strength (@ref Editor::GenerateFxTiles()). They're added to
    the tiles of this level by default, or to any tile set, and become the brush.

@subsection editor-tiles-palette Palette

<b>Palette</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu shows the palette in use --- the default tile set's, unless the level has its
own. Only the level's own palette can be changed; it starts as a copy.

-   Click picks an entry, drag or <kbd>Shift</kbd> picks a range. The first picked entry is edited as RGB, HSV,
    `#RRGGBB` or with the color picker.
-   <b>Gradient</b> fills the range evenly between its ends. <b>From Tile Set</b> restores the tile set's colors.
-   The level, sprites included, updates live while the window is open.
-   <b>Into Tile Set</b> makes the palette the default tile set's palette (or that of a new tile set), and the level no
    longer has its own.
-   <b>Import</b> reads JASC (.pal), GIMP (.gpl), Adobe (.act) or PNG (an indexed image's palette, or a 16 × 16 grid of
    colors) into the level's own palette; entry 0 stays transparent. <b>Export</b> picks the format by the file
    extension (@ref Editor::PaletteFile).
-   Usage shows how many pixels of the level's 8-bit tiles use the entry. <b>Unused</b> crosses out entries no tile
    uses (sprites may still use them), <b>Next</b> jumps to the next one.
-   The arrows move the picked entries, <b>Swap</b> swaps the first and the last. With <b>Keep the look of the
    level</b>, pixels are remapped so the level looks the same; otherwise pixels show whatever color is now at their
    index. Either way it's one undo step.

@section editor-terrain Terrain tools

-   <b>Paint Tiles of Stamp at Random</b> (<kbd>Z</kbd>) --- the brush, rectangle and fill put a random tile of the
    stamp in each cell, chosen by the cell position, so painting over it again doesn't change it. Tiles repeated in the
    stamp come up more often, and an empty spot in the stamp leaves the cell alone. <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>
    rerolls.
-   <b>Smart Brush</b> (<kbd>T</kbd>) --- left button paints ground, right button removes it. Each cell gets the tile
    that levels of this tile set use most for the same 8 neighbors, so blocks get their tops, edges and corners
    automatically (@ref Editor::SmartTileModel). On first use the levels are scanned (progress in the status bar) and
    the result is cached in @cpp "Cache/Editor/Adjacency/" @ce until a level changes. Animated tiles, tiles of other
    tile sets, and tiles that already fit their neighbors are left alone.
-   <b>Choose Tiles by Their Neighbors</b> (<b>Edit</b> menu) re-picks the selected tiles the same way.
    <b>Fill Selection with Ground</b> makes the selection solid ground.
-   <b>Beautify Selection</b> (<b>Edit</b> menu) keeps the collision exactly, but replaces each tile with one of the
    same mask (flipped or not) that fits its neighbors better. Tiles without collision are kept.
-   <b>Find and Replace</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>) --- finds a tile from the brush (same flip or any) in the
    current layer or all of them. <b>Select</b> selects the matches, <b>Replace</b> swaps in another brush tile keeping
    flip and translucency. Events are found by kind and replaced by another kind with default parameters, keeping
    difficulties and flags. One undo step each.

@subsection editor-terrain-rules Terrain rules and prefabs

<b>Terrain Rules...</b> in the <b>Edit</b> menu (@ref Editor::TerrainRules) shows the tile used for each of the 47 ways
the surrounding cells can be solid (a corner counts only if both sides next to it are solid) and lets you override it:

-   Click assigns the brush tiles; several tiles alternate by cell position. Right-click restores the learned tile.
    Overridden tiles are framed and used first by the Smart Brush, the Ground Line tool and neighbor-based picking.
-   Tiles of extra tile sets work too --- they're found by tile set name in any level.
-   A tile set can have several <b>terrains</b> (ground, wall, cloud, ...); the one chosen at the top is used.
-   <b>Slopes</b> tags the brush tile as a slope with surface heights at both edges, or excludes it from ground, for
    tiles whose masks are misleading. The Ground Line tool uses the tags.
-   <b>Try It</b> is a small sandbox where clicks toggle cells and tiles are picked as the tools would.

<b>Prefabs...</b> in the <b>Edit</b> menu stores named stamps for the default tile set, usable in any level of it
(@ref Editor::TilePrefab): <b>Save Brush</b> saves the brush, <b>Use</b> makes a prefab the brush. Animated tiles are
left out.

Rules and prefabs are stored per tile set in `Tilesets/<name>.rules`, a JSON file found like tile sets and saved into
the user content. The game ships rules for `castle1`, `diam1` and `jungle2`, as a starting point
(`Content/Tilesets/*.rules`).

@subsection editor-terrain-ground-line Ground Line tool

The <b>Ground Line</b> tool (<kbd>P</kbd>) lays ground along a line. Clicks add points (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd> snaps to tile
corners), the line runs left to right, and the right button lays it as one undo step.

-   Tiles along the line get the ground pieces whose masks follow it best, flat or sloped (@ref Editor::TileShapes),
    from those the levels use with the same neighbors. Below, it's filled down to existing ground (at most 6 tiles),
    and edges are picked as by the Smart Brush.
-   Kinds: <b>Ground</b> below the line, <b>Ceiling</b> above it, a floating <b>Band</b> as thick as the depth, or a
    closed <b>Shape</b>. <b>Smooth curve</b> makes it a curve through the points. Options also apply to the last line.
-   The line is kept with the level (@ref Editor::TerrainSpline, editor-only chunk @cpp "spln" @ce) and stays
    editable: drag a point (ground follows live, is laid on release), click the line to add a point,
    <kbd>Shift</kbd>+click to remove one, right-click the line to remove its ground.
-   The laid collision surface is drawn over the line, green where it matches and red where it misses.
-   Relaying restores tiles it wrote that are still unchanged; tiles edited by hand since are left alone.
-   <b>Make Tiles Along Ground Line</b> (<b>Edit</b> menu) cuts the most common solid tile along the last line into
    tiles of this level, so collision follows the line exactly --- even for tile sets without slopes.
-   <b>Make Ground Line from Selection</b> (<b>Edit</b> menu) traces a line along the top of the selected ground, so
    existing ground can be reshaped by dragging it.

@section editor-events Events

Everything that isn't a tile --- starts, enemies, pickups, springs, warps, tile behaviors --- is an event. Events sit
on tiles of the sprite layer, one per tile, regardless of which layer is being edited.

The <b>Events</b> tab in the left panel lists them by category, with a search box. Clicking one selects it and the
<b>event tool</b> (<kbd>Q</kbd>). Events the game doesn't use are found only by searching.

@subsection editor-events-placing Placing and selecting

-   Click an empty tile to place the event, drag to keep placing. <kbd>Shift</kbd> places over existing events.
-   Click an event to select it, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+click to toggle it in the selection, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+drag over empty
    tiles to select a rectangle. Drag selected events to move them.
-   Right button erases. <kbd>Alt</kbd>+click picks an event with all its parameters for placing elsewhere.
-   <kbd>Del</kbd>, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd>, <kbd>X</kbd> and <kbd>V</kbd> work on selected events; pasted events
    are placed by a click, keeping their arrangement. A warp pasted with its target, or a trigger with its tiles, gets
    a fresh identifier so the copy works on its own; a lone part keeps its identifier and joins the original.

The right panel shows the parameters of the selected events, or of the event to place. Changes apply to all selected
events of the first one's type; difficulties and flags apply to all. <b>Raw parameters</b> at the bottom show all 16
bytes.

Single selected events have white <b>handles</b> for parameters with a position in the level --- light radii, bridge
width, chain length of spike balls and platforms, pole position, new water level, camera limit edges. Dragging one is
a single undo step (@ref Editor::EventGizmos). A camera limit's right edge dragged to the end of the level stays at
the end even if the level is resized.

In the <b>Edit</b> menu:

-   <b>Select Events of the Same Kind</b> selects all events like the first selected one.
-   <b>Copy Event to Every Tile Like Its Own</b> places the selected event on every sprite layer tile showing the same
    tile and flip and having no event --- e.g. place a hook once and copy it to all hook tiles.

Two tools place events too:

-   <b>Measure</b> (<kbd>U</kbd>) --- drag to measure in pixels and tiles (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd> snaps to tile centers). It
    draws the arcs of a walking jump, a running jump and Spaz's double jump as the original game computes them
    (`Legacy` constants of @ref Actors::Player), and tells which reach the end. Walls and ceilings aren't considered.
-   <b>Events Along Path</b> (<kbd>K</kbd>) --- places the event evenly along a path, e.g. a row or arc of coins.
    Clicks add points (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd> snaps to tile centers), right button places them. Its window sets the spacing,
    smooth curve, and whether events go off-grid on the path or on the tiles it crosses.

@subsection editor-events-display How events look

Events are drawn as the objects they create, with a corner mark in their category's color. Other events are boxes in
that color with their name, shrunk, wrapped or cut to initials as needed. Badges:

-   white and dark squares, bottom right --- difficulties, if not all three,
-   blue square, top right --- the object respawns,
-   purple square, bottom left --- multiplayer only.

Warps are linked to their targets with blue arrows; a warp without a target is crossed out in red. A selected trigger
event shows the others of the same trigger, a light shows its reach, a water level change shows the new level, and a
camera limit darkens the area the camera can't reach. Events are dimmed while another layer is edited, and hidden
under other tools unless <b>Show Events</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>E</kbd>) is on.

@subsection editor-events-offgrid Off-grid events

Off-grid events --- objects at any position, as levels made in MLLE can have --- are kept, shown faded, and played as
the game plays them (@ref Events::EventMap): spawned at their exact position, gone for good once collected or
destroyed, and respawned after a checkpoint. Starts and warp targets keep their exact position; tile modifiers apply to
the tile they're in.

-   <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+click places the event off the grid.
-   Click selects one (edit in the right panel, <kbd>Del</kbd> removes it, copying makes an on-grid event), drag moves
    it (<kbd>Shift</kbd> snaps to tile centers), right button erases, <kbd>Alt</kbd> picks it.
-   <b>Snap Off-Grid Events to Grid</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu moves them onto their tiles in one step, except where
    the tile already has an event.

@subsection editor-events-catalog Event catalog

Event descriptions are compiled in, in @ref Editor::EventCatalog, split by category into
@cpp Editor/Events/EventCatalog.*.cpp @ce. They describe parameters as the game reads them, not as the level converter
writes them (it reorders and scales them). Each event has a type, a stable key, a name and description, a category,
parameters and a preview sprite.

A parameter (@ref Editor::EventParamField) occupies some bits of the 16 parameter bytes and has an editor kind ---
number, yes/no, options, flags, another event, text, or a warp, trigger or text identifier. Bytes no parameter covers
are preserved.

@ref Editor::ResolveEventPreview() mirrors how objects pick their look from parameters (food kind, gem color, spring
direction...). Horizontal springs without a set direction turn away from the adjacent wall, upward springs stand on
the bottom of their tile, and bridges are shown at their length. Sprites are drawn among the layers as in the game,
while marks, badges and links stay on top.

@subsection editor-events-extensions Events described outside the game

A level script can describe events the game doesn't know, or uses differently, with lines in its `.j2as` file --- one
event per line in JSON, ignored by the script itself:

@code{.cpp}
#pragma editor-event {"type": 300, "key": "MyThing", "name": "My Thing", "category": "enemy", "preview": {"metadata": "Enemy/Turtle", "animation": 1}, "params": [{"name": "Speed", "type": "uint", "offset": 0, "bits": 4, "max": 15, "default": 3}, {"name": "Mode", "type": "enum", "offset": 0, "shift": 4, "bits": 2, "options": ["Walk", "Fly", "Swim"]}]}
@endcode

Event packs for all levels are `.json` files in `Editor/Events/` of the content or the user content --- a single event,
an array, or an object with an `"events"` array. Priority: level script, then event packs, then built-in events.
They're read when a level is opened or saved under another name; <b>Read Event Descriptions Again</b> in the
<b>Level</b> menu reloads them. Supported fields are listed at @ref Editor::EventCatalog::LoadExtensions(); errors go
to the log. An undescribed event is shown as its number with raw parameters only.

@section editor-checking Checking the level

In the <b>Level</b> and <b>View</b> menus:

-   <b>Statistics</b> --- counts per difficulty: enemies, bosses, gems and coins by value (including those in crates and
    barrels), food, carrots, extra lives, ammo, containers, checkpoints, warps, exits, generators and character starts
    (a missing start is red). Multiplayer-only events aren't counted (@ref Editor::LevelStatistics).
-   <b>Check Level</b> (@ref Editor::CheckLevel()) --- lists likely problems: no Jazz start (other characters fall back
    to it), no multiplayer start in a multiplayer level, warps without targets, coin warps needing more coins than
    exist, empty texts, triggers that change nothing or are never set, rolling rocks and triggers without each other,
    out-of-range parameters, unknown events, more than one sky layer, no reachable exit, and pickups that may be out of
    reach. Errors come first in red. <b>Show</b> jumps to the event, <b>Check Again</b> reruns it; the window stays
    open while fixing.
-   <b>Show Where the Player Can Get</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd>) --- tints green every tile
    reachable from the start, brighter near the start. It uses the multiplayer minimap's route tracer
    (@ref Multiplayer::ComputeReachableTiles()), which walks, jumps, swims, climbs and uses springs, poles, tubes and
    warps like the original game. It's conservative, so some routes may be missing, and it doesn't update live ---
    toggle it again after changes.

A camera limit narrower than the game screen is widened to the left by the game; the editor shows the resulting area
lighter, with a thinner edge.

@section editor-playing Playing the level

The level can be played straight from the editor, saved or not. The view flies to the player position, the game is
revealed in a growing ring, and a bar at the top slides in to get back. Music continues throughout.

-   <b>Play</b> (<kbd>F5</kbd>) --- continues the last play-test where it was left, if the level wasn't changed. If it
    was, the level restarts where the player was, with the same lives, health, score, weapons, gems and shield, and
    collected, destroyed or triggered things stay so --- except in changed cells and events. A resized sprite layer
    starts fresh. On consoles with little memory, the play-test isn't kept and only the position carries over.
    Without a previous play-test, the level starts at its start.
-   <b>Play Here</b> (<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F5</kbd>) --- starts at the pointer (from the key) or the view center (from
    the menu or toolbar), at the nearest spot the player fits, or the level start if there's none.
-   <b>Play from Start</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F5</kbd>).
-   <b>Play from Marker</b> (<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>F5</kbd>) --- starts at the green play-start flag set by <b>Set Play
    Start Marker Here</b> (<kbd>F6</kbd>), handy for testing one section repeatedly. The marker is editor-only.

The character and difficulty are chosen in the <b>Play</b> menu. <kbd>F5</kbd>, <b>Back to Editor</b> in the pause menu,
reaching the exit, or a game over all return to the editor, with the view where the player was. The next level is
never loaded and nothing is saved.

The <b>Play</b> menu can also play <b>local splitscreen multiplayer</b> --- Cooperation, Battle, Team Battle, Race,
Treasure Hunt or Capture the Flag with 1 to 4 players --- to test multiplayer starts, laps and bases
(@ref Editor::PlayTestMpLevelHandler). <b>Alone</b> switches back. Extra players take the next characters, and Play Here
places them side by side. Changing the mode or player count restarts the play-test.

After a play-test, the player's path is drawn over the level, blue to gold, thick where fast, with a red mark at
deaths and a white one where the test ended. <b>Show Play Path</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>L</kbd>) toggles it.

@section editor-files Files

-   <b>Save</b> --- writes the level back to its file. Levels in @cpp "Cache/" @ce are saved as a new file instead,
    since the cache is rebuilt from the original levels.
-   <b>Save As</b> --- saves into the user content @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref paths) @m_endspan, or into
    @cpp "Content/" @ce if writable; a game level goes into the same episode by default. If the level also exists
    elsewhere, the editor tells which one the game will load (user content, then @cpp "Content/" @ce, then
    @cpp "Cache/" @ce). The level script (@cpp ".j2as" @ce) is copied along.
-   <b>Export</b> --- saves for publishing, without editor-only data, after checking for problems such as a missing
    start. <b>Don't allow editing</b> in the file dialog sets @ref LevelFlags::NotEditable: the game plays the level,
    but the editor won't open it and the pause menu won't offer <b>Edit This Level</b>. It only prevents accidents ---
    anyone can clear the bit --- so keep your editable copy.

Files are written to a temporary file next to the target and moved into place when complete, so a failed save never
damages a level.

@subsection editor-files-recovery Recovery

While a level has unsaved changes, a recovery copy is written into @cpp "Editor/Recovery/" @ce in the configuration
directory every minute and when the application is suspended. It's removed when the level is saved or changes are
discarded. If a copy newer than the level is found when opening it, the editor offers to restore it.

@subsection editor-files-web In a browser

The web version can't open local files directly: <b>From This Computer</b> in the open dialog uploads a level, and
saving also downloads it. Settings and recovery copies are kept in browser storage (@ref Editor::WebFileBridge).

@subsection editor-files-settings Settings

Editor settings are stored in @cpp "Jazz2.Editor.json" @ce in the configuration directory: grid visibility, tile picker
width, interface scale (@cpp 0 @ce = by window size), @cpp "Layout" @ce (@cpp 0 @ce by window size, @cpp 1 @ce desktop,
@cpp 2 @ce compact), @cpp "Expert" @ce, floating panel positions, play-test character, difficulty and players, and
recent files (listed in the <b>File</b> menu). The view state of each level is stored in the level itself, in the
@cpp "edoc" @ce chunk.

@section editor-formats Level and tile set formats

All level reading and writing --- by the converter, the game and the editor --- goes through
@ref Formats::LevelReader and @ref Formats::LevelWriter, and tile sets through @ref Formats::TileSetFile.
@ref Formats::LevelFile describes the level layout.

The editor holds a level as an @ref Editor::LevelDocument. Loading and saving a level is lossless --- the file comes
out byte for byte the same. Tiles are referenced by tile set and index, so adding a tile set or an animated tile
doesn't change any layer.

@subsection editor-formats-chunks Chunks

A level can carry optional data at the end of its body, in chunks tagged by four characters. Readers skip unknown
chunks, so newer levels still load in older versions. Tags starting with a lowercase letter are editor-only: the game
never reads them and export drops them.

Tag | Contents
--- | --------
@cpp "lnam" @ce | Layer names, one per layer, in layer order
@cpp "elay" @ce | Layer editor state (hidden, locked), one byte per layer
@cpp "rcov" @ce | Path of the level a recovery copy belongs to (recovery copies only)
@cpp "PRVW" @ce | Preview image for the custom level list, kept on export: @cpp u16 @ce width and height, then @cpp R, G, B @ce bytes row by row
@cpp "anno" @ce | Bookmarks and notes: @cpp varuint @ce count, then each: @cpp i32 @ce X and Y in pixels, @cpp u8 @ce bookmark number (0 = note), @cpp varuint @ce length and text
@cpp "edoc" @ce | Editor view state, written on every save so the level reopens as it was left (@ref Editor::DocumentViewState): @cpp u8 @ce version, @cpp u16 @ce flags (camera, grid, events, play path, flat layers, play settings, play-start marker), @cpp f32 @ce center X, Y and zoom relative to the game's, @cpp i32 @ce layer, @cpp u8 @ce mask mode and picker panel, @cpp u8 @ce character, difficulty, game mode and players, @cpp i32 @ce play-start marker X and Y
@cpp "spln" @ce | Ground lines: @cpp varuint @ce count, then each: @cpp i32 @ce layer, @cpp u8 @ce depth, @cpp u8 @ce kind (ground, ceiling, band, shape), @cpp u8 @ce flags (1 = smooth), @cpp varuint @ce point count and @cpp f32 @ce X and Y of each in layer pixels, @cpp varuint @ce count of written tiles and for each @cpp i32 @ce X and Y, then the written and previous tile, each as @cpp u16 @ce index, @cpp u8 @ce source and @cpp u8 @ce flags

@subsection editor-formats-verify Verifying the formats

The `verify-formats` command of @ref asset-packer "AssetPacker" reads every level and tile set in a directory and
writes it back into memory, which must give the same file byte for byte. Files written by other tools, which compress
differently, must read back to the same content instead.

@code{.sh}
AssetPacker verify-formats <directory>
@endcode

@section editor-testing Testing the editor

Debug builds have an editor self-test. `/editor-selftest` loads every level in @cpp "Cache/Episodes/" @ce (or a given
directory) into an @ref Editor::LevelDocument and checks that:

-   saving it gives the same file,
-   editor data survives saving and loading, and export drops it,
-   a random sequence of changes, undone, restores the original, and redone, the changed level.

It first validates the @ref editor-events-catalog "event catalog" (parameters don't overlap, fit their bits, and
defaults match), then logs everything in the levels' events the catalog doesn't expect --- unknown events, values out
of range, and unclaimed bits (mostly converter bytes the game ignores). Results go to the log, then the application
quits.

@code{.sh}
Jazz2 /editor-selftest
@endcode

Debug builds also run `/console` commands once the editor is open, in order, so it can be driven without input ---
e.g. to take screenshots. Positions are in window pixels.

Command | What it does
------- | ------------
`/jump <x> <y> [scale]`, `/fly <x> <y> <scale>` | Move the camera instantly, or fly it
`/zoom <scale>`, `/fit` | Zoom, fit the whole level
`/layer <index>`, `/hide <index>`, `/layers` | Edit a layer, hide it, log the layers
`/paint <layer> <x> <y> <[source:]tile> [width] [height]` | Paint tiles of the default tile set, or of a source numbered as in `/addtiles`
`/undo`, `/redo`, `/action <id>` | Undo, redo, run a menu action
`/click <x> <y>`, `/rclick <x> <y>`, `/drag <x1> <y1> <x2> <y2>`, `/hover <x> <y>`, `/wheel <x> <y> <amount>` | Pointer input
`/key <name>` | Press `enter`, `escape`, `delete`, `f5`, `left`, `right`, `up`, `down`, `space`, `ctrl`, `shift` or `alt`
`/keydown <name>`, `/keyup <name>` | Hold or release a key (held keys are read once per frame)
`/gamepad <x> <y>` | Simulate gamepad use with its cursor at a position
`/renderer <type>` | Set the current layer's renderer, see @ref Tiles::LayerRendererType
`/addtiles <source> <image.png> [all] [dither]` | <b>Add Tiles from Image</b>; `all` keeps duplicates; source is a number as in `/applytiles`, `level`, or `new:<name>`
`/fxtiles <source> <kind> <width> <height>` | <b>Generate Effect Tiles</b> into a source as in `/addtiles`; kind is an @ref Editor::FxTileKind
`/newtileset <name> <tiles> [extra\|default\|save]` | <b>New Tile Set</b>
`/importtileset <image.png> <name> [extra\|default\|save] [own\|true\|match\|reduce] [dither]` | <b>New Tile Set from Image</b>, colors kept by default
`/exporttileset <image.png>` | <b>Export Tile Set Image</b>
`/applytiles <source> [new name\|-] [palette]` | <b>Save Tile Changes into Tile Set</b>; source 0 is the default tile set, 1+ the extra ones; `palette` saves the level palette too
`/tileoverride <tile ID> <index>` | Fill a tile with one palette entry and make it solid, in this level only
`/palette <entry> <rrggbb>` | Set a level palette entry (the level gets its own palette)
`/animframe <index> <frames>` | Set an animated tile's frame time
`/placeevent <key> <x> <y>` | Place a catalog event with default parameters on a tile
`/placeoffgrid <key> <x> <y> [delay]` | Place an off-grid catalog event at a pixel position; with a delay in seconds, as a generator
`/brush <key>`, `/selectevent <x> <y> [add]`, `/events` | Choose the event to place, select (or add) the event of a tile, log the selection and brush
`/texts`, `/findevents <key>` | Log the level texts with control characters, or up to 20 events of a kind
`/pngtest <directory>` | Read every PNG in a directory, compare with a same-named `.rgba` file if present, write it to `out/` and read it back
`/save <path>`, `/export <path>`, `/exportplayonly <path>`, `/autosave` | Save, export, export as not editable, write a recovery copy
`/playring <progress>` | Freeze the play transition at a point between 0 and 1
`/exitlevel` | Leave the played level through its exit
`/wait <frames>`, `/quit` | Wait before the next commands, quit

@code{.sh}
Jazz2 /edit prince/01_castle1 /console "/jump 320 320 2" /console "/paint 2 8 8 5 6 4" /console "/wait 30" /console "/quit"
@endcode

*/

}
